#+title:      Caestria Agent Integration Into Cyberdeck

* Purpose

This file is the contract between AI agents and the AIU Cyberdeck:
how an agent connects, what it may call, what it must record, and
which capabilities stay dormant until a connection exists.  It is
documentation, not configuration: no mechanism tag, no code blocks,
never loaded by the boot.  Human and agent readers share it.

* Connection

A session is connected when the `my/atlas-agent-endpoint' option holds
an endpoint URL.  While it is nil, every boot emits one warning — "No
agent connected" — and the AI features below stay dormant.  The
`my/atlas-agent-status' command reports the state at any time.

Rules for a connected session:

- Read through the API catalog (`my/atlas-api-catalog'), never by
  reaching into module internals.  The catalog splits reads from
  writes; agents use reads freely and writes deliberately.
- Results cross the boundary as plain data (plists, lists, strings)
  or JSON (`my/atlas-api-json').  No closures, buffers, or markers
  cross it.
- Every write carries provenance: model, timestamp, and source hash
  land in a PROVENANCE drawer entry next to the value written.

* Capabilities an agent may use

Reads: query AIUs by filter, fetch an AIU by id, describe a schema
(fields and includes), list the catalog.  Writes: set a drawer field
(with provenance), append an id link.  Queries compose with and, or,
not over tags, title text, and file age, with sort and limit; saved
queries persist by name and org-babel blocks execute them in place.

* Capabilities that stay dormant

Semantic similarity (endpoint plus model options, background sidecar
index, similar-note hints only, never links) and AI drawer proposals
(plan buffer with per-item accept, explicit accept writes through the
standard drawer primitive) both require a configured provider.  With
none configured they are inert code paths, verified dormant by the
absence of their options.

Termux note: on-device embedding needs a running compatible service
or a remote endpoint; nothing here assumes either exists.  Both
features degrade to nil — hints show "dormant", proposals report
"provider dormant or failed" — and every failure path was built to
say so instead of throwing.

* Boundaries (what agents must not do)

- Never edit generated artifacts (early-init, foundation-init, the
  tangled loader) or anything under admin/ and WIP/.
- Never rename drawers, tags, or schema keys outside the tag
  manager's preview machinery.
- Never invent storage: new persisted state goes in cache-grade
  sidecars (disposable, rebuildable) or in drawers visible next to
  the data.  No second truths.
- Keybindings are owned by the keyboard system; agents define
  commands, never bindings.

* Module map (all under atlas-engine/)

persistence-hygiene, atlas-transactions, inline-hashtag,
unlinked-mentions (plus hint overlays), tag-manager, sync-hygiene,
tag-hierarchy, automation-engine, agent-api, query-language,
discovery-stream, node-view, tag-stream, data-doctor,
semantic-similar, ai-proposals, promote-tool.  Each file is one
tagged load unit with its own identity drawer; each commit in
history carries exactly one of them, so any single addition
reverts alone.
